    "description": "Iran’s petroleum-processing infrastructure includes crude-oil refineries and facilities processing gas-field condensate. Reported capacity, actual throughput, and the production of particular fuels measure different aspects of this system.",
    "tags": ["Refineries", "Petroleum processing", "Abadan", "Condensate", "Industrial capacity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:10:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:10:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Refineries: Capacity, Condensate and Fuel Supply | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s refinery capacity statistics, the role of condensate processing, and the difference between installed capacity and available fuel supply."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "processing-network",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different facilities, different measures",
        "content": "Iran’s petroleum-processing system includes crude-oil distillation and condensate processing. EIA identifies Persian Gulf Star as a facility processing condensate from South Pars and describes a new distillation unit added at Abadan in March 2023. Its assessment also notes continued gasoline imports despite processing investments. More installed processing equipment does not, by itself, demonstrate that domestic demand for every fuel is met. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reported-capacity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A consistent published capacity series",
        "content": "OPEC’s 2025 Annual Statistical Bulletin reports Iran’s refinery capacity at 2,201 thousand barrels per day in 2020, rising to 2,237 thousand barrels per day in 2022 and remaining at that reported level through 2024. The accompanying series preserves those published values. It measures capacity rather than actual refinery intake or fuel production. [s2]\n\nThe complete table footnotes could not be inspected because direct retrieval of the PDF failed. Consequently, the series should be treated as OPEC’s reported capacity measure, without assuming that its coverage matches another publisher’s crude-distillation or condensate-processing totals. It should not be used to calculate a utilization rate against a differently defined throughput series. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "capacity-trend",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran’s reported refinery capacity, 2020–2024",
          "dataset_id": "opec-refinery-capacity",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["reported-capacity"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Thousand barrels per day",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "OPEC reports a small increase between 2020 and 2022, followed by unchanged capacity through 2024. These figures do not establish throughput, product quality, or subsequent operating availability."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "facility-identification",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why precise facility identification matters",
        "content": "Fuel depots, oil refineries, and gas-treatment plants should be distinguished when assessing disruption. On June 15, 2025, S&P Global reported a fire at the Shahran storage depot while describing the nearby Tehran oil refinery as unaffected and operating normally, citing Iran’s oil ministry news service. The same report separately discussed fires at gas-treatment plants. Its account supports a dated distinction between affected facilities; it does not establish their condition at the October 2026 cutoff. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
